When a board drains, run a configured command. karr does not know and must not know what it does.

This is the seam that keeps karr generic. In the fleet this design came from, `on_drained` starts a release gate that builds a distribution, installs it, tests every dependent against it and raises version requirements — none of which belongs in a kanban tool.

An empty board is also not the same as finished work: the gate may fail and file new tickets, at which point the board is no longer drained. The hook has to tolerate being the reason more work appears.

Design: `karr get-refs refs/karr-foundation/spec/fleet-execution.md`
Implemented. Commit ec3099b (local, not pushed).

DECISIONS

1. Trigger is NOT outcome eq 'idle'. The briefing said idle is the drained case; it is not. A drain that empties a board ends on 'progress' (the last iteration made progress, then the loop exits on an empty actionable set) -- checked against _drain_repo before writing anything. 'idle' is "the agent did nothing and grabbed nothing", i.e. a board that was already quiet. Keying the hook on idle would have meant it never fires on the case it exists for. The trigger is the observable fact instead: after the drain, _has_actionable_tasks is false. That is also the only formulation that survives mode: ticket, which does one card and returns.

2. A drain that ended in common-error/error does not count as drained: a rate-limited agent leaves a board that looks exactly like one it worked through, and foundation is about to cool that board down because it does not believe the run.

3. Reuses Runner::_run_command, with two new options (role, max_runtime). Reason: the hook must not survive a SIGTERM, and _run_command is where setpgid, the TERM-then-KILL of the group and the _live_agent registration the shutdown handler reads all live. A second door would have been a second place to get #148/#163 right. What it does NOT share is the identity -- role => 'hook' sets KARR_ROLE=hook, so the hook's own karr writes land in a different activity log and can never be read as the agent's engagement evidence for an auto-block, and PROMPT is left empty because the prompt is the agent's instruction. No ticket, no agent definition, so no render, no result-JSON classification, no error-pattern scan, no agent-availability marking. Own budget via on_drained_max_runtime, because how long an agent may run says nothing about how long a release gate may.

4. Runs under the board's .karr.lock (between _drain_repo and _release_lock), in its own try: a gate that builds and installs out of the working tree must not have another tick's agent walk into it, and a hook that dies must not carry _release_lock with it.

5. THE LOOP QUESTION. An empty board is not finished work; the hook may file tickets and so be the reason the board is no longer drained. The cycle hook-files / agent-works / board-drains / hook-runs-again is the point, so it is bounded, not forbidden. Two guards in .karr.state, both overridden by --force:
  (a) the same board state is never asked twice (on_drained_hash = the fingerprint that triggered the run). Without it a repository nobody touches starts a release gate on every cron tick for ever, because a drained board stays drained. (a) alone does NOT stop the loop -- every hook-filed ticket changes the hash -- which is why there is a (b).
  (b) on_drained_rounds counts consecutive runs in which the hook itself put work back on the board, measured by the board fingerprint before/after and never by asking the hook. A run that leaves the board alone -- the gate that finally passed -- clears it. At on_drained_max_rounds (default 3, 0 disables) the hook is suppressed with a line in .karr.log naming the reason.
  --force overrides both because they are statements about board state, which is what --force is documented to override; and unlike the cooldown and agent availability the cap is not time-bounded and does not end by itself, so it needs a way out and the operator is it.

6. Config: on_drained / on_drained_max_runtime / on_drained_max_rounds, .karr first then config file, like every other key. on_drained: "" turns a fleet-wide hook off for one board.

